      "writtenOn": "2019-06-26T16:23:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Obviously, there are lots of overlaps between the native GPIO framework and IO_Expander. How much thought did you put in more unified approach (meaning extending the native GPIO framework)? If we add a whole new set of APIs, it\u0027ll impact code size.\n\nAt this point, I\u0027m not convinced this is the right direction.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 10,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "298693410b30e5738daf331193b9bdeb8e6c4c0d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a655fead_30154d8d",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 1212794
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-26T19:11:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My first thought for how to do something more unified would be to have IOEX() in gpio.inc add an entry into \"struct gpio_info gpio_list[]\", storing the ioex_port in a new field added to struct gpio_info.\n\nUnfortunately, the implementation of gpio_set/get_level() etc is spread among chip/*/gpio.c so all of those would need to be touched to add handling for the new IOEX signals. Maybe all those API functions should move up to common/gpio.c and then call down into either a per-chip driver (for GPIO signals) or the ioexpander_drv (for IOEX signals).",
      "parentUuid": "1e1cf9f8_4dde34b6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 7,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 10,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "298693410b30e5738daf331193b9bdeb8e6c4c0d",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
